<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José Fernando de Abascal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1743">1743</a>–<a href="/years/1821">1821</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José Fernando de Abascal was a Spanish officer who served as viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> from 1806 to 1816. Making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> the centre of royalist resistance to the Spanish American independence movements, he sent armies against the patriots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, where his troops helped bring down the Patria Vieja in 1814.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-fernando-de-abascal.jpg" alt="José Fernando de Abascal" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pedro Díaz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_D%C3%ADaz_-_Jos%C3%A9_Fernando_de_Abascal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Fernando de Abascal y Sousa was born on 3 June 1743 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, into a noble family, and entered the army at the age of 19. After 20 years of service he was promoted to colonel, and later, in the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, to brigadier. In 1796 he took part in the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> against the British. Named commanding general and intendant of Nueva Galicia in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> three years later, he took up that office in 1800. In 1804 he was appointed viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>, but never took possession, as the same year he was named viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Taken prisoner by the British on his voyage from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Abascal reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> only in 1806. Once in office he promoted educational reforms, reorganised the army and put down local rebellions, and the medical school of San Fernando was founded in 1810. The last cargo of slaves to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> landed in 1806. He ordered mass vaccination against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> in Lima, without much success, as the vaccine had to be paid for and vested interests kept it as a source of revenue. In 1812 he backed Francisco Uville's plan to bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engines</a> by Richard Trevithick to pump out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines of Cerro de Pasco.</p><p>When revolution broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> on 25 May 1810, he reoccupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-paz">La Paz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Charcas</a> and joined them again to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>, as he did <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>. A firm supporter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a>, he still sent money and materiel to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-cadiz">Cortes of Cádiz</a> in the fight against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, but he worked to keep the liberal Spanish Constitution of 1812 from being applied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, and the revolts that followed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacna">Tacna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arequipa">Arequipa</a> were repressed. The distance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the wars let him govern almost independently.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Abascal was created marqués de la Concordia in 1812. In 1816 he was recalled at his own request and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where he was succeeded as viceroy by General Joaquín de la Pezuela. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> on 30 June 1821, at the age of 79, having made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> the centre of royalist reaction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “José Fernando de Abascal y Sousa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…a governing junta formed on 18 September 1810, a date still celebrated as the national holiday, opening the period known as the Patria Vieja, in which creole leaders such as José Miguel Carrera and Bernardo O'Higgins quarrelled while Viceroy <b>José Fernando de Abascal</b> sent royalist forces from Peru.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the wars of independence Viceroy <b>José Fernando de Abascal</b> turned Peru into the royalist base of South America, sending armies that crushed revolutionaries in Upper Peru and Quito and reconquered Chile after the Battle of Rancagua in 1814.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Divisions between the Carreras and Bernardo O'Higgins weakened the patriots as the viceroy of Peru, <b>José Fernando de Abascal</b>, sent troops in 1813.</blockquote><a href="/state/patria-vieja">Patria Vieja</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.36,-5.85&amp;year=1793" aria-label="Oviedo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of José Fernando de Abascal"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4637 3858h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4637 3858h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.36,-5.85&amp;year=1793">What was here in 1793? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
